<!-- Keys to ADD to the Info.plist that `npx cap add ios` generates at ios/App/App/Info.plist.
     Not a replacement file: Capacitor's own keys (CFBundleIdentifier, the launch storyboard, the
     Capacitor bridge settings) must stay. Merge these in.

     No double hyphen appears in any comment here: XML forbids it inside a comment, and a plist
     that will not parse is a build failure, not a warning.

     Every key here is one I am confident about. The Background Assets entitlement is deliberately
     NOT here; see README.md. -->

<!-- Export compliance, answered once instead of at every submission.
     The app uses HTTPS and nothing else, which is the standard exemption. Declaring it here means
     App Store Connect stops asking on each upload; leaving it out means answering the question by
     hand every time and getting it wrong once. -->
<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
<false/>

<!-- The name under the icon. "Rounds Codex" is 12 characters and will not truncate on a home
     screen; the App Store name is set separately in App Store Connect. -->
<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
<string>Rounds Codex</string>

<!-- iPad is a supported destination, so all four orientations. A reference app locked to portrait
     on an iPad reads as a phone app that was not thought about; and the galleries are 2:3
     portrait artwork that people will absolutely turn sideways to read. -->
<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
<array>
  <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
  <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
  <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
  <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
</array>

<!-- iPhone: no upside-down, which is Apple's own convention for phones. -->
<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
<array>
  <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
  <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
  <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
</array>

<!-- NOT PRESENT ON PURPOSE, and each absence is a decision:

     NSCameraUsageDescription, NSMicrophoneUsageDescription, NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription,
     NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription
        The app uses none of these. An unused purpose string is not harmless; it puts a
        capability on the App Store privacy page that you then have to justify, and it invites a
        reviewer question you have no reason to answer.

     NSAppTransportSecurity
        The default is correct. Everything the app touches is HTTPS. Adding an exception here is
        the single most common way an otherwise fine submission acquires a rejection.

     UIFileSharingEnabled / LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace
        The app neither imports nor exports documents. The gallery PDF is handed to the system
        share sheet, which needs no entitlement.

     UIRequiresFullScreen
        Left unset, so the app supports iPad multitasking. Setting it true would opt out of Split
        View, which Apple discourages and reviewers notice on a universal app. -->
